INT. WIZARD'S ABANDONED LIBRARY - TWILIGHT

A circular chamber at the top of a leaning tower. Shelves spiral to a ceiling lost in cobweb. Dust hangs in the last red light from a cracked rose window. On a lectern in the centre lies an enormous book, its pages stirring though there is no wind.

The silence is old and settled.

A heavy door groans. Boots on stone.

ORRIN THISTLEDOWN, sixties, a thin man in a patched travelling cloak, enters leaning on a gnarled staff. He is breathing hard from the stairs. A wiry grey beard, spectacles tied with twine. He pauses, taking in the room as one takes in a grave.

ORRIN
(softly)
Well. You kept it tidy, old friend.

He crosses to the lectern. Brushes dust from the open page with the back of his hand. Reads. His lips move.

From his satchel he draws a folded letter, brown at the edges. He smooths it on the lectern beside the book.

ORRIN (V.O.)
(reading the letter, in his younger voice)
"Orrin, if you read this, I am gone, and the tower is yours. The book will not open for anyone but you. Do not be afraid of it. It is only lonely."

Orrin lowers the letter. He looks up at the rose window, where the last sun is bleeding out.

ORRIN (CONT'D)
(to the book)
Lonely. Aye. So are we.

He sets his staff against the shelves, rolls up his sleeves, and turns the first page. A faint golden glow rises from the paper and catches in his spectacles.

ORRIN (CONT'D)
(a small, wondering laugh)
There you are.

The pages begin to turn on their own, one after another, patient as breathing. Orrin pulls up a stool and sits.
